          %p (…)
          %h3#CohomotopyChargeMap Cohomotopy charge map
          %p
            The
            %em Cohomotopy charge map
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
            that assigns to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/configuration+space+of+points"} configuration of points
            their total
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/charge"} charge
            as measured in
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cohomotopy"} Cohomotopy
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+cohomology"} cohomology theory
          %p
            This is alternatively known as the “electric field map” (
            %a{:href => "#Salvatore01"} Salvatore 01
            following
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Segal73"} Segal 73, Section 1
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Knudsen18"} Knudsen 18, p. 49
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Kallel98"} Kallel 98
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D \in \mathbb{N}
            the
            %em Cohomotopy charge map
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+function"} continuous function
          #eq:CohomotopyChargeMapOnEuclideanSpace.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (9)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Conf
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi D
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi cc
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
